Found in a bug hunt; reproduced, and re-verified independently.

Cmd/Edit.pm calls apply_status_change at line 244 but applies --release at
line 285 -- forty lines later. So the require_claim guard in
Role/TaskMutation.pm:295-298

    die "Status '$new_status' requires --claim\n"
        if $self->store->status_requires_claim($new_status)
        && !( defined $claimant && length $claimant )
        && !$task->has_claimed_by;

is satisfied by a claim that the same command is about to throw away.
--release also skips check_claim (Edit.pm:241), which is its documented job.

Net effect: a card lands in a require_claim column with NO claim on it -- a
state `karr move` and `karr edit --status` both refuse to create by any
other route.

Reproduction:

    $ karr move 1 in-progress
    Status 'in-progress' requires --claim
    1 of 1 ids failed                              exit=1

    $ karr edit 1 --claim agent-a
    $ karr edit 1 --status in-progress --release
    Updated task 1: Ticket one                     exit=0

    $ karr show 1 --json
    {... "status":"in-progress", ...}              # no claimed_by key

`karr edit 2 --status in-progress --claim agent-b --release` produces the
same result in ONE command with no prior claim needed. kanban-md rejects
--claim together with --release outright (cmd/edit.go:128-130) and runs its
equivalent guard outside the release branch (internal/board/mutate.go:442-445).

Cost: require_claim is the guarantee karr's multi-agent coordination rests
on (ticket #55). An unclaimed card in in-progress is work in flight for
nobody -- invisible to pick, and it is what karr-foundation's engagement and
auto-block logic reads. Reachable by accident through the perfectly
reasonable `karr edit N --status in-progress --release`.
## Fix: edit --status X --release no longer slips through require_claim

**Files changed:** lib/App/karr/Cmd/Edit.pm
**Test added:** t/154-edit-release-require-claim.t
**Option chosen:** Option A (reject --claim + --release; clear --release before the require_claim guard runs).

Option A matches kanban-md's design (cmd/edit.go:128-130 reject --claim + --release; internal/board/mutate.go:442 fire the post-edit require_claim check regardless of release). It was the safer choice: 'karr edit --status X --release' is what an operator would type, and it should refuse cleanly rather than silently produce an invalid state.

The two changes:

1. Reject --claim and --release together at the top of execute() as a usage error (exit 2). kanban-md rejects the pair at the flag layer too; we match.
2. Move the --release block from the bottom of the per-id callback to right before apply_status_change(). The require_claim guard in Role::TaskMutation's apply_status_change is satisfied by an existing claim; clearing the claim first lets the guard see the post-release state and refuse a status change into a require_claim column. kanban-md's equivalent check (validateEditPost) fires after applyFn regardless of release, so the guard is no longer bypassable.

'karr move' has no --release option, so the third bullet of the ticket ('also check whether karr move ID --status X --release has the same shape') is a no-op for karr; the bug is edit-only.

The regression test t/154-edit-release-require-claim.t has three subtests:
- --claim and --release together are rejected as a usage error (the ticket's single-command reproduction: 'karr edit N --status X --claim NAME --release').
- The ticket's two-step reproduction (claim first, then 'karr edit N --status in-progress --release') is now refused with the same require_claim message 'karr move' gives, leaving the task untouched.
- Unclaimed-then-release still works: --release alone clears the claim; --status in-progress without --claim is still refused; --status in-progress --claim NAME still lands the card and records the claim.

